                <pb facs="0227" n="125" sameAs="frus:frus1927v03/pg125"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">865.3311/57: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Fletcher</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Rome</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-04-09T11:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 9,
                                1927—11 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received April 9—10:30
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>51. Your 29, April 8, 4 p.m. Press despatches put the cart before the horse. The
                    Fascist newspapers immediately attributed the accident to an anti-Fascist plot;
                    hence my alternative reference to possible sabotage, promising thorough
                    investigation. It was necessary and had a good effect. Now, however, in the
                    light of Pinedo’s statement which has been published here it is generally
                    understood that there was no anti-Fascist connection.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Fletcher</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
